{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W4pqiZAqt4Fwt6UsH2ZZJT","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# docs/stack.ini — the kit meta-repo's own product toolchain, declared once.\n2\t#\n3\t# The \"product\" this repo builds is the kit under project-trajectory/; its tests\n4\t# live in tests/ (they bootstrap a real scaffold and exercise every script). This\n5\t# is the meta-repo's SELF-adoption of the process it ships — IMPROVEMENT_PLAN.md\n6\t# Thread 47. scripts under project-trajectory/scripts/check.py read this file.\n7\t\n8\t[paths]\n9\tsrc = project-trajectory/scripts\n10\ttests = tests\n11\t\n12\t[product]\n13\tformat = {py} -m ruff format --check {src} {tests}\n14\tlint = {py} -m ruff check {src} {tests}\n15\t# `-n auto` runs the suite across all cores via pytest-xdist (WI-075). Every test\n16\t# is isolated in tmp_path and the meta-tree readers are read-only, so the suite is\n17\t# xdist-safe; subprocess coverage (tests/conftest.augment_env) holds per-worker\n18\t# (measured combined total 90.8%, unchanged from serial). Measured 2026-07-22 on\n19\t# a 24-core box: ~250 s parallel for the full 1374-test suite (~200 s for the\n20\t# smoke tier); the coverage step runs several times longer. These are snapshots,\n21\t# not contracts — re-measure locally before budgeting against them (the prior\n22\t# ~65 s figure predated the v4 dispatch/integrate suite growth and misled\n23\t# session budgeting for weeks; repo-review 2026-07-21 M-24).\n24\t# The `smoke` tier stays as declared; the coverage args below still apply.\n25\ttest = {py} -m pytest -q -n auto\n26\t\n27\t[tiers]\n28\tsmoke = -m smoke\n29\tfull = -m \"not release\"\n30\trelease =\n31\tall =\n32\t\n33\t[coverage]\n34\t# Subprocess coverage IS wired (Thread 47 Phase 6). This suite exercises the kit\n35\t# scripts as SUBPROCESSES (bootstrap a temp scaffold, run the real command), which\n36\t# `--cov` would otherwise miss, so tests/conftest.run_py starts coverage in each\n37\t# child (COVERAGE_PROCESS_START + tests/_cov/augment_env) and .coveragerc's\n38\t# [paths] folds each temp-scaffold */scripts/ copy back onto the source tree, while\n39\t# its [report] omit drops the temp-dir test fixtures so the number is the PRODUCT's.\n40\t# Product coverage measures ~91% on the PINNED toolchain (requirements-dev.txt,\n41\t# WI-104: pytest-cov ~=7, uniform now the kit floor is 3.11 — WI-262). The floor is 85 — a real\n42\t# REGRESSION SENSOR, not a formality: WI-105 (the 2026-07-12b review's M9). The pin\n43\t# fixed the flaky combine race that had degraded the measurement to ~82% (children\n44\t# silently lost from the combine on the old-local pytest-cov 4.1.0), but an 80 floor\n45\t# would have passed that ~82% silently — \"1.83 points of plumbing luck\". At 85 a\n46\t# renewed silent subprocess-coverage loss (~91% -> ~82%) turns the gate RED instead,\n47\t# with ~6 points of headroom for legitimate variation on both pinned Pythons. NB: an\n48\t# UNPINNED toolchain still reads the degraded ~82% and now fails here — that red is\n49\t# the intended nudge to `sh scripts/dev-setup.sh --install` onto the pinned tools.\n50\tthreshold = 85\n51\t# `--cov-report=json` (writes coverage.json at the repo root) feeds the\n52\t# [step:module-coverage] per-module floors below (WI-279 / repo-review 2026-07-22\n53\t# M-4): the global {coverage} floor stays the aggregate backstop, and the JSON\n54\t# gives the per-module comparator its numbers. coverage.json is gitignored.\n55\targs = --cov={src} --cov-report=term-missing --cov-report=json --cov-fail-under={coverage}\n56\t\n57\t[step:dupes]\n58\t# Duplicate-code gate (WI-078; check.py extra_steps). Fails on any copy-paste\n59\t# block of >= --min-tokens significant tokens between a file-PAIR not recorded\n60\t# in the census docs/dupes-allow (the default --allowlist path). The census\n61\t# grandfathers the current F5 small-helper duplication + the known intra-file\n62\t# debris; new duplication between an unlisted pair turns the G3 gate RED. Runs\n63\t# at G3 with the other product-layer quality gates (format/lint/tests).\n64\tcommand = {py} project-trajectory/scripts/check_dupes.py --src {src}\n65\tgates = G3\n66\tlayer = product\n67\t\n68\t[step:module-coverage]\n69\t# Per-module coverage floors (WI-279; repo-review 2026-07-22 M-4). The global\n70\t# [coverage] threshold above is one aggregate number, so a heavily-tested\n71\t# generator can subsidize thin coverage in a security/process boundary\n72\t# (agent_session.py, subagent_gate.py, plan_runner.py) while the headline still\n73\t# passes. check_coverage.py compares the per-file percents in coverage.json (the\n74\t# [coverage] --cov-report=json above) against the honest per-module minimums in\n75\t# docs/coverage-floors, failing G3 on a breach; the global --cov-fail-under stays\n76\t# the backstop. Runs after tests+coverage (check.py orders extra steps after the\n77\t# test step), so the JSON exists. `--tier {tier} --skip-tiers smoke` makes the\n78\t# smoke tier SKIP without reading the report, and check.py run-scopes coverage.json\n79\t# (clears a stale copy before the plan), so a prior full-tier report can never be\n80\t# graded as a current pass at the smoke tier (repo-review 2026-07-22 REVIEW-A) —\n81\t# the two together are the belt-and-suspenders fix. Raise a floor (in docs/\n82\t# coverage-floors) only alongside the focused tests that earn it.\n83\tcommand = {py} project-trajectory/scripts/check_coverage.py --tier {tier} --skip-tiers smoke\n84\tgates = G3\n85\tlayer = product\n86\t# Serialize AFTER tests+coverage (it reads that step's coverage.json): this\n87\t# repo's CI runs `check.py --jobs 0` (parallel), where an un-laned step would\n88\t# race the producer, find no JSON yet, and SKIP — silently NOT enforcing the\n89\t# floors. `lane` puts it in the tests+coverage lane so it runs once the JSON\n90\t# exists (a no-op at --jobs 1, which is already ordered).\n91\tlane = tests+coverage\n92\t\n93\t[arch-map]\n94\t# Symbol map over project-trajectory/scripts (Phase 6 authors docs/architecture.md).\n95\tmode = symbols\n96\t\n97\t[generated]\n98\t# The parallel-dispatch integrator's auto-resolution allowlist (WI-235): a\n99\t# composition conflict CONFINED to one of these generated artifacts is resolved\n100\t# by REGENERATING from the merged sources instead of parking. agent_dispatch.py\n101\t# reads THIS section (the integrate worktree's own copy); an ABSENT section falls\n102\t# back to the byte-identical built-in defaults, a MALFORMED row fails closed to\n103\t# park. Each row: <path> = <kind> [| <BEGIN> | <END>]; kinds are\n104\t# trajectory | okf | status | archmap; a \"/\"-terminated path is a prefix. The\n105\t# skills index is deliberately absent (its per-agent copies are hand-authored\n106\t# source that must park, not regenerate — WI-231).\n107\tPROJECT_STATE.html = trajectory\n108\tdocs/okf/ = okf\n109\tdocs/architecture.md = archmap | <!-- BEGIN GENERATED MODULE MAP --> | <!-- END GENERATED MODULE MAP -->\n110\tdocs/status.md = status | <!-- BEGIN GENERATED STATUS --> | <!-- END GENERATED STATUS -->\n111\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f51d775d-365f-4a06-87af-1743fc0e0a5e","uuid":"f132964b-044e-4b39-96cb-28c8461cd68f","timestamp":"2026-07-24T08:13:32.516Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template-trains\\p0-g3-WI-282-eb40\\docs\\stack.ini","content":"# docs/stack.ini — the kit meta-repo's own product toolchain, declared once.\n#\n# The \"product\" this repo builds is the kit under project-trajectory/; its tests\n# live in tests/ (they bootstrap a real scaffold and exercise every script). This\n# is the meta-repo's SELF-adoption of the process it ships — IMPROVEMENT_PLAN.md\n# Thread 47. scripts under project-trajectory/scripts/check.py read this file.\n\n[paths]\nsrc = project-trajectory/scripts\ntests = tests\n\n[product]\nformat = {py} -m ruff format --check {src} {tests}\nlint = {py} -m ruff check {src} {tests}\n# `-n auto` runs the suite across all cores via pytest-xdist (WI-075). Every test\n# is isolated in tmp_path and the meta-tree readers are read-only, so the suite is\n# xdist-safe; subprocess coverage (tests/conftest.augment_env) holds per-worker\n# (measured combined total 90.8%, unchanged from serial). Measured 2026-07-22 on\n# a 24-core box: ~250 s parallel for the full 1374-test suite (~200 s for the\n# smoke tier); the coverage step runs several times longer. These are snapshots,\n# not contracts — re-measure locally before budgeting against them (the prior\n# ~65 s figure predated the v4 dispatch/integrate suite growth and misled\n# session budgeting for weeks; repo-review 2026-07-21 M-24).\n# The `smoke` tier stays as declared; the coverage args below still apply.\ntest = {py} -m pytest -q -n auto\n\n[tiers]\nsmoke = -m smoke\nfull = -m \"not release\"\nrelease =\nall =\n\n[coverage]\n# Subprocess coverage IS wired (Thread 47 Phase 6). This suite exercises the kit\n# scripts as SUBPROCESSES (bootstrap a temp scaffold, run the real command), which\n# `--cov` would otherwise miss, so tests/conftest.run_py starts coverage in each\n# child (COVERAGE_PROCESS_START + tests/_cov/augment_env) and .coveragerc's\n# [paths] folds each temp-scaffold */scripts/ copy back onto the source tree, while\n# its [report] omit drops the temp-dir test fixtures so the number is the PRODUCT's.\n# Product coverage measures ~91% on the PINNED toolchain (requirements-dev.txt,\n# WI-104: pytest-cov ~=7, uniform now the kit floor is 3.11 — WI-262). The floor is 85 — a real\n# REGRESSION SENSOR, not a formality: WI-105 (the 2026-07-12b review's M9). The pin\n# fixed the flaky combine race that had degraded the measurement to ~82% (children\n# silently lost from the combine on the old-local pytest-cov 4.1.0), but an 80 floor\n# would have passed that ~82% silently — \"1.83 points of plumbing luck\". At 85 a\n# renewed silent subprocess-coverage loss (~91% -> ~82%) turns the gate RED instead,\n# with ~6 points of headroom for legitimate variation on both pinned Pythons. NB: an\n# UNPINNED toolchain still reads the degraded ~82% and now fails here — that red is\n# the intended nudge to `sh scripts/dev-setup.sh --install` onto the pinned tools.\nthreshold = 85\n# `--cov-report=json` (writes coverage.json at the repo root) feeds the\n# [step:module-coverage] per-module floors below (WI-279 / repo-review 2026-07-22\n# M-4): the global {coverage} floor stays the aggregate backstop, and the JSON\n# gives the per-module comparator its numbers. coverage.json is gitignored.\nargs = --cov={src} --cov-report=term-missing --cov-report=json --cov-fail-under={coverage}\n\n[step:dupes]\n# Duplicate-code gate (WI-078; check.py extra_steps). Fails on any copy-paste\n# block of >= --min-tokens significant tokens between a file-PAIR not recorded\n# in the census docs/dupes-allow (the default --allowlist path). The census\n# grandfathers the current F5 small-helper duplication + the known intra-file\n# debris; new duplication between an unlisted pair turns the G3 gate RED. Runs\n# at G3 with the other product-layer quality gates (format/lint/tests).\ncommand = {py} project-trajectory/scripts/check_dupes.py --src {src}\ngates = G3\nlayer = product\n\n[step:module-coverage]\n# Per-module coverage floors (WI-279; repo-review 2026-07-22 M-4). The global\n# [coverage] threshold above is one aggregate number, so a heavily-tested\n# generator can subsidize thin coverage in a security/process boundary\n# (agent_session.py, subagent_gate.py, plan_runner.py) while the headline still\n# passes. check_coverage.py compares the per-file percents in coverage.json (the\n# [coverage] --cov-report=json above) against the honest per-module minimums in\n# docs/coverage-floors, failing G3 on a breach; the global --cov-fail-under stays\n# the backstop. Runs after tests+coverage (check.py orders extra steps after the\n# test step), so the JSON exists. `--tier {tier} --skip-tiers smoke` makes the\n# smoke tier SKIP without reading the report, and check.py run-scopes coverage.json\n# (clears a stale copy before the plan), so a prior full-tier report can never be\n# graded as a current pass at the smoke tier (repo-review 2026-07-22 REVIEW-A) —\n# the two together are the belt-and-suspenders fix. Raise a floor (in docs/\n# coverage-floors) only alongside the focused tests that earn it.\ncommand = {py} project-trajectory/scripts/check_coverage.py --tier {tier} --skip-tiers smoke\ngates = G3\nlayer = product\n# Serialize AFTER tests+coverage (it reads that step's coverage.json): this\n# repo's CI runs `check.py --jobs 0` (parallel), where an un-laned step would\n# race the producer, find no JSON yet, and SKIP — silently NOT enforcing the\n# floors. `lane` puts it in the tests+coverage lane so it runs once the JSON\n# exists (a no-op at --jobs 1, which is already ordered).\nlane = tests+coverage\n\n[arch-map]\n# Symbol map over project-trajectory/scripts (Phase 6 authors docs/architecture.md).\nmode = symbols\n\n[generated]\n# The parallel-dispatch integrator's auto-resolution allowlist (WI-235): a\n# composition conflict CONFINED to one of these generated artifacts is resolved\n# by REGENERATING from the merged sources instead of parking. agent_dispatch.py\n# reads THIS section (the integrate worktree's own copy); an ABSENT section falls\n# back to the byte-identical built-in defaults, a MALFORMED row fails closed to\n# park. Each row: <path> = <kind> [| <BEGIN> | <END>]; kinds are\n# trajectory | okf | status | archmap; a \"/\"-terminated path is a prefix. The\n# skills index is deliberately absent (its per-agent copies are hand-authored\n# source that must park, not regenerate — WI-231).\nPROJECT_STATE.html = trajectory\ndocs/okf/ = okf\ndocs/architecture.md = archmap | <!-- BEGIN GENERATED MODULE MAP --> | <!-- END GENERATED MODULE MAP -->\ndocs/status.md = status | <!-- BEGIN GENERATED STATUS --> | <!-- END GENERATED STATUS -->\n","numLines":111,"startLine":1,"totalLines":111}}}
